#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121040 :tutorial/content "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span}\n\t\\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}0} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 1} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}\n$$\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n$$\n\\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}0} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 1} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}\n$$\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{red}- 1} \\\\ {\\color{red}3}\\end{array} \\right]} }_{\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = \n\t(-1) \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0}\\end{array} \\right]} }_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + \n\t3 \\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}0} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]} }_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\n$$\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n$$\n\\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}1} \\\\ {\\color{blue}0}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\color{blue}0} \\\\ {\\color{blue}1}\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}\n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ff21f5d-5007-5b74-b135-bdb080fd5541" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121044 :tutorial/content "As we saw, swapping two rows does not preserve the determinant. However, the only difference is the sign of the determinant changes. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, swapping any two rows of a matrix changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite one. That is to say, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=-det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ ◻ & ◻ & … & ◻ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f151b612-1bdb-5723-af50-a48439141fbc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3236 :tutorial/title "Switching Two Rows Changes the Sign of the Determinant"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121045 :tutorial/content "As we have seen, multiplying a row by a number does not preserve the determinant. However, it does affect the determinant in a predictable way: multiplying a row by a number scales the determinant by that number as well.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2\\cdot 1 & 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 4=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}2} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}2} \\cdot  2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}2} \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nIn general, multiplying a row of a matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the row of the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ k△ & k△ & … & k△ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}=k\\cdot det\\begin{bmatrix}⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮ \\\\ △ & △ & … & △ \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & … & ⋮\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09c28da6-5340-56f9-bbf5-427a824977ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3237 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Row By a Number Scales the Determinant By That Number"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121058 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6318f9cd-930b-5b7b-acc7-c27b5b241825" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3519 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121061 :tutorial/content "Let's check what happens if we multiply the entire matrix (not only one row) by a number. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 3=-2 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}3\\cdot 1 & 3\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 4\\end{vmatrix}=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\cdot 12-6\\cdot 9=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in the above example, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}{\\color{red}3} \\cdot  1 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  2 \\\\ {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  3 & {\\color{red}3} \\cdot  4\\end{array}} \\right| = {\\color{red}3}^2 \\cdot \\left| {\\begin{array}{cc}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{array}} \\right|.\n$$\n\nThe above is equivalent to multiplying both rows of the matrix by $3$. Multiplying the first row scales the determinant by $3$, and then multiplying the second row scales it by $3$ once more.\n\nIn general, multiplying an entire $n \\times n$ matrix by a number multiplies the determinant by that number to the power of $n$. That is to say, we can factor out the common multiplier from the entire matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot A) &={k}^{n}\\cdot det(A),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "1c99dfd1-b583-5247-9ac5-c2ada47c0ee9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3880 :tutorial/title "Multiplying a Matrix By a Scalar"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121064 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span}\n\t\\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0f5ce4-8098-51b1-a25e-8cb4d84505c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3945 :tutorial/title "Finding a Basis by Identifying Pivot Columns"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121065 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span}\n\t\\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_3} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B} = \\Bigg\\{ \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_1}, \n\t\t\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\color{black}\\mathbf{a}_2} \n\t\\Bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n{\\color{black}x_1} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{rblack}x_2} \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} = \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3}.\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}1 & - 1 & - 1 \\\\ - 2 & 3 & 7\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left[\n\t{\\begin{array}{cc|c}\\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & 0 & {\\color{red}4} \\\\ 0 & \\bbox[3pt,lightgray]{ {\\color{blue}1}} & {\\color{red}5}\\end{array}}\n\t\\right].\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n$$\n\\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_3} = {\\color{red}4}\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1} + {\\color{red}5}\\cdot \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 3\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "14b13853-b5c6-5bc9-825c-a11cd19dff93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3968 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121068 :tutorial/content "Note that the number of pivot columns always coincides with the number of non-zero rows in the corresponding row echelon form matrix.\nSo, technically, \n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *pivot columns* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.\n\nOr, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *non-zero rows* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c062d466-946c-526b-aba8-0a0895671218" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4151 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Definition of the Rank"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\\textrm{I.}\\:\\: \\Bigg\\{ \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}, \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} \\Bigg\\}\n}\n{\\color{red}\n\\qquad\\qquad\n\\textrm{II.}\\:\\: \\Bigg\\{ \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_1}, \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_2} \\Bigg\\}\n}\n$$\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\\right\\}\n$$\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} - {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + x_2 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}+ x_3 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124298} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436343}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124486 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124299} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdde35e9-7101-55f2-8452-ec1ef2d17d54" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124907} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323710} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360491} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420713}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Matrix Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124487 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124300} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99ce8f12-ee79-5384-8eb9-e9bfbb2534c5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048524}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407779}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Polynomial Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124302} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406882} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186664052}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124303} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555871}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124403} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360515} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186664050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125760 :tutorial/content "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "939069a1-d4fd-51b0-8c49-c1cdde533481" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3197 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125761 :tutorial/content "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n*A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x_1 \\ne 0,$ and we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n.\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." :tutorial/id #uuid "7600b92d-52bf-5c9a-bd48-5e9d12eb0d25" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3198 :tutorial/title "An Important Property of Linearly Dependent Sets"}]}
